{"ex-207-1":{"number":"1","title":"Calculating momenta","question":"<p>Fishes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> swim towards each other. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of mass 5 kg swims at 0.8 m s<sup>−1</sup> towards the left. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of mass 0.6 kg swims at 4 m s<sup>−1</sup> towards the right. Find their individual momenta and total momentum.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/e21a513d8b14c050bf3c2081.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:384.2px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Take the direction towards the left as positive.</p><p>Apply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 5 × 0.8 = 4 kg m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>The momentum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 4 kg m s<sup>−1</sup> (leftwards).</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.6 × (−4) = −2.4 kg m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>The momentum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 2.4 kg m s<sup>−1</sup> (rightwards).</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 4 + (−2.4) = 1.6 kg m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>Their total momentum is 2.4 kg m s<sup>−1</sup> (leftwards).</p>","tips":"<p>Editorial check</p><p>The final sentence in the printed solution gives 2.4 kg m s<sup>-1</sup>. Its preceding calculation correctly gives a total momentum of 1.6 kg m s<sup>-1</sup> to the left.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 207  |  Source PDF p. 5","source":{"name":"207 Examples.docx","sha256":"f64059e99bd3353230405ed45f03e7681e57f19a62eac691b4fac3fa0321567c","block":2},"id":"ex-207-1","chapter":"207"},"ex-207-2":{"number":"2","title":"Skaters pushing each other","question":"<p>Mark and Joanna are standing on ice. To get them moving, Mark pushes Joanna with a constant force for 0.5 s. Joanna moves at 0.9 m s<sup>−1</sup> towards the right after the push. Mark’s mass is 60 kg and Joanna’s mass is 55 kg. Assume that the friction acting on them by the surface of the ice is negligible. What is Mark’s velocity after pushing Joanna?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/9b9cff00a0893ea5d7d3ce9d.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:260.5px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Take the direction towards the right as positive.</p><p>By the law of conservation of momentum,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>J</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>J</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>J</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>J</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>0 + 0 = 60<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + 55 × 0.9</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = −0.825 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>Mark’s velocity is 0.825 m s<sup>−1</sup> (leftwards) after pushing Joanna.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Skill</b></p><p>Problem-solving</p><p>Applying conservation of momentum ① Define the positive direction. ② Check the external forces acting on the system. Conservation of momentum is valid only if the external net force is zero. ③ Set an equation with the conservation of momentum. ④ Solve the equation.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>u<sub>M</sub> = 0 and u<sub>J</sub> = 0</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 207  |  Source PDF p. 11","source":{"name":"207 Examples.docx","sha256":"f64059e99bd3353230405ed45f03e7681e57f19a62eac691b4fac3fa0321567c","block":17},"id":"ex-207-2","chapter":"207"},"ex-207-3":{"number":"3","title":"Trolleys sticking together after collision","question":"<p>Trolley <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of mass 0.5 kg moves at 2 m s<sup>−1</sup> towards the left on a smooth horizontal track. After colliding with stationary trolley <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, they move together at 0.4 m s<sup>−1</sup> towards the left.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/5d5b3c99fc9350cc89269504.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:408.1px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of trolley <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Find the total KE of the trolleys before and after the collision.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Take the direction towards the left as positive.</p><p>By the law of conservation of momentum,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>)<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>0.5 × 2 + 0 = (0.5 + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>) × 0.4</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2 kg</p><p>(b) Total KE before collision = 0 + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 0.5 × 2<sup>2</sup> = 0.5 J</p><p>Total KE after collision = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × (0.5 + 0.5) × 0.5<sup>2</sup> = 0.125 J</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Since the track is horizontal and smooth, the external net force acting on the system of colliding trolleys is zero.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The two trolleys moving together after the collision can be considered as a single object.</p><p>Editorial check</p><p>The question states a final speed of 0.4 m s<sup>-1</sup>, while the original diagram labels it 0.5 m s<sup>-1</sup>. Using the question&#x27;s value gives a mass of 2 kg for B, total initial KE of 1.00 J and total final KE of 0.200 J. The original diagram and printed solution are retained above.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 207  |  Source PDF p. 12","source":{"name":"207 Examples.docx","sha256":"f64059e99bd3353230405ed45f03e7681e57f19a62eac691b4fac3fa0321567c","block":34},"id":"ex-207-3","chapter":"207"},"ex-207-4":{"number":"4","title":"Trolleys moving in opposite directions","question":"<p>Trolley <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of mass 10 kg moves at 0.8 m s<sup>−1</sup> towards the right. Trolley <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of mass 6 kg moves at 2 m s<sup>−1</sup> towards the left. After colliding head-on, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> moves at 0.1 m s<sup>−1</sup> towards the right. Assume the friction acting on the trolleys is negligible.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/fa4d0fa43a7191fa8bf62397.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:471.4px\"/></p><p>(a) What is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>’s velocity after the collision?</p><p>(b) What is the loss in the total KE of the trolleys? What kind of collision is this?</p><p>(c) If the collision is completely inelastic, what is the loss in the total KE of the trolleys?</p>","answer":"<p>Take the direction towards the right as positive.</p><p>(a) By the law of conservation of momentum,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>10 × 0.8 + 6 × (−2) = 10 × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + 6 × 0.1</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = −0.46 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>’s velocity is 0.46 m s<sup>−1</sup> (towards the left).</p><p>(b) Total KE before collision = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 10 × 0.8<sup>2</sup> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 6 × 2<sup>2</sup> = 15.2 J</p><p>Total KE after collision = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 10 × 0.46<sup>2</sup> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 6 × 0.1<sup>2</sup> = 1.09 J</p><p>Loss in total KE = 15.2 − 1.09 = 14.1 J</p><p>This is an inelastic collision.</p><p>(c) By the law of conservation of momentum,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> )<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>10 × 0.8 + 6 × (−2) = (10 + 6) × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = −0.25 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>Total KE after collision = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × (10 + 6) × 0.25<sup>2</sup> = 0.5 J</p><p>Loss in total KE = 15.2 − 0.5 = 14.7 J</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 207  |  Source PDF p. 14","source":{"name":"207 Examples.docx","sha256":"f64059e99bd3353230405ed45f03e7681e57f19a62eac691b4fac3fa0321567c","block":55},"id":"ex-207-4","chapter":"207"},"ex-207-5":{"number":"5","title":"Force on the ejected gas","question":"<p>A rocket car ejects 30 kg of hot gas at 500 m s<sup>−1</sup> backwards in 1 s when it starts from rest. Find the force acting on the gas by the car during this period of time. Assume that this force is constant.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/99edc249bf6eac8addc3a208.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:447.1px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Take the direction of ejection of the gas as positive.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 15 000 N</p><p>The force acting on the gas by the car is 15 000 N.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The force acting on the gas by the car is the net force on the gas.</p>","think":"<p>What is the forward force acting on the rocket car by the gas?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>15 000 N forwards. By Newton’s third law, the force exerted by the gas on the car is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the backward force exerted by the car on the gas.</p>","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 207  |  Source PDF p. 25","source":{"name":"207 Examples.docx","sha256":"f64059e99bd3353230405ed45f03e7681e57f19a62eac691b4fac3fa0321567c","block":79},"id":"ex-207-5","chapter":"207"},"ex-207-6":{"number":"6","title":"Change in momentum","question":"<p>Tommy pushes his toy car, which is initially at rest, with a constant horizontal force of 2 N towards the left. The friction acting on the toy car is 1 N. Find the momentum of the toy car after 0.5 s.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/33666ca5f7b8c975198dc8b1.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:236.6px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Take the direction towards the left as positive.</p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> − <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = (2 − 1) × 0.5 + 0 = 0.5 kg m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>The momentum of the toy car after 0.5 s is 0.5 kg m s<sup>−1</sup> (towards the left).</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 207  |  Source PDF p. 26","source":{"name":"207 Examples.docx","sha256":"f64059e99bd3353230405ed45f03e7681e57f19a62eac691b4fac3fa0321567c","block":94},"id":"ex-207-6","chapter":"207"},"ex-207-7":{"number":"7","title":"F−t graph and average force","question":"<p>The figure shows how the net force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> acting on a ball varies with time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/784bd1f7b0aa20c38c93c698.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:288.2px\"/></p><p>In this period of time,</p><p>(a) what is the maximum net force acting on the ball?</p><p>(b) what is the change in momentum of the ball?</p><p>(c) what is the average net force acting on the ball?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) 4 N</p><p>(b) Change in momentum = area under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>−<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph</p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>+</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= 1.2 kg m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>(c) Average net force = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>change in momentum</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>time</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2.4 N</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 207  |  Source PDF p. 29","source":{"name":"207 Examples.docx","sha256":"f64059e99bd3353230405ed45f03e7681e57f19a62eac691b4fac3fa0321567c","block":103},"id":"ex-207-7","chapter":"207"},"ex-207-8":{"number":"8","title":"Impact force on a falling egg","question":"<p>An egg of mass 0.05 kg falls vertically onto the ground. It moves at 4 m s<sup>−1</sup> just before hitting the ground. It does not rebound after the impact. The impact time is 4 ms. Find the average impact force acting on the egg by the ground.</p>","answer":"<p>Take the upward direction as positive.</p><p>Average net force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 50 N</p><p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> − <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 50 + 0.05 × 9.81 = 50.5 N</p><p>The average impact force is 50.5 N (upwards).</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/3de3cadfe32eb159f2d31c1a.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:103.6px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>When an object hits a surface or another object vertically, the net force equals to the resultant of the impact force and its weight.</p>","think":"<p>Assume the egg can withstand an impact force of 60 N without breaking. Will the egg break in the above case? Why?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>The average impact force, 50.5 N, is below 60 N. If the impact force is approximated as constant at this value, the egg will not break. Strictly, the average alone is insufficient to decide: the actual peak force could exceed 60 N even when the average is smaller. A definite prediction requires the maximum force or a force–time curve.</p>","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 207  |  Source PDF p. 30","source":{"name":"207 Examples.docx","sha256":"f64059e99bd3353230405ed45f03e7681e57f19a62eac691b4fac3fa0321567c","block":117},"id":"ex-207-8","chapter":"207"},"ex-207-9":{"number":"9","title":"Falling object","question":"<p>A dry cell of mass 23 g falls from a height of 30 m (about the height of 10/F). When it collides with the ground, the impact time is 5 ms. Assume the collision is completely inelastic, i.e. the dry cell does not rebound, and the air resistance is negligible. Find the average force acting on the ground by the dry cell during the impact.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a7f879a651237daefa7569ac.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:186.3px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Take the downward direction as positive.</p><p>Consider the motion during the fall.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = +9.81 m s<sup>−2</sup>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = +30 m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = ?</p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math> + 2<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math> = 0<sup>2</sup> + 2 × 9.81 × 30</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 24.26 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>During the impact,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = −111.6 N</p><p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> − <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p>−111.6 = 0.023 × 9.81 − <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 112 N</p><p>By Newton’s third law of motion, the average force acting on the ground by the dry cell during the impact is 112 N (downwards).</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/d76ee9f6f2b25f5f13117380.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:103.6px\"/></p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 207  |  Source PDF p. 35","source":{"name":"207 Examples.docx","sha256":"f64059e99bd3353230405ed45f03e7681e57f19a62eac691b4fac3fa0321567c","block":134},"id":"ex-207-9","chapter":"207"},"ex-207-10":{"number":"10","title":"Motion of a bouncing ball","question":"<p>A ball of mass 0.2 kg is released from rest below a motion sensor (Fig a). It hits the ground and bounces several times. Figure b shows the ball’s <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>−<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph, with downwards taken as positive.</p><p>Ignore the size of the ball. Assume the ball only moves along the vertical direction.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/d9f7d2ccce71213e00bd8d44.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:292.4px\"/></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/9d8903f096612e3e1e4626cc.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:511.1px\"/></p><p>(a) At what time does the ball</p><p>(i) hit the ground the first time,</p><p>(ii) reaches its maximum height during its first rebound?</p><p>(b) Describe the motion of the ball corresponding to the part from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> in the graph.</p><p>(c) Estimate the acceleration due to gravity from the graph. Assume the air resistance is negligible.</p><p>(d) Estimate the average net force acting on the ball during its first impact with the ground.</p><p>(e) Is the total mechanical energy of the ball conserved during each impact? Explain your answer.</p><p>(f) Sketch the displacement−time graph of the ball from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2.0 s.</p><p>No calculation is needed.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) (i) At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.9 s</p><p>(ii) At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 1.3 s</p><p>(b) From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, the ball moves upwards and slows down.</p><p>At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, it reaches its maximum height and is momentarily at rest.</p><p>From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, it speeds up from rest and moves downwards.</p><p>(c) During the fall from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.5 s to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.9 s,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = slope of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>−<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> graph</p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= 10 m s<sup>−2</sup></p><p>The acceleration due to gravity is 10 m s<sup>−2</sup> (downwards).</p><p>(d) During the impact from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.9 s to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 1.0 s,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>−</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>= −14 N</p><p>The average net force acting on the ball during its first impact is 14 N</p><p>(upwards).</p><p>(e) No. The ball has a lower speed after an impact than before the impact, showing that some KE has been lost.</p><p>(f)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/394158e4a5decccc5ac5e124.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:261.5px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The velocity of the ball drops abruptly when it hits the ground.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The velocity of the ball is zero when it reaches its maximum height.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 207  |  Source PDF p. 36, 37","source":{"name":"207 Examples.docx","sha256":"f64059e99bd3353230405ed45f03e7681e57f19a62eac691b4fac3fa0321567c","block":152},"id":"ex-207-10","chapter":"207"}}